<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghazni</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghazni is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> on the road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>. From 977 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a>, whose ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni">Mahmud of Ghazni</a> filled it with palaces, mosques and the spoils of his Indian raids, making it a centre of Persian culture. The Ghurids sacked it in 1150 and later ruled from it; two Ghaznavid minarets still stand.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghazni.jpg" alt="Ghazni" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US State Department (US Embassy in Kabul) a US Government organization, hence PD-USGov applies · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citadel_of_Ghazni,_seen_from_Tapa_Sardar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ghazni stands on a plateau about 2,219 m above sea level, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>–Kandahar Highway, which follows paths that have served for thousands of years as the main roads between Kabul, Kandahar and Gardez. In antiquity it was a small market town. It was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus II</a> in the 6th century BCE and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> in 329 BCE, when it was called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in Opiana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the fall of the Greco-Bactrian kingdoms the region became the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabulistan">Zabulistan</a>, a kingdom that controlled the trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. From the 5th to the 7th centuries it was ruled by the Nezak Huns, and the area was a major centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, and also of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visited in 644 and described a city he called Jaguda. Arab armies brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to the region in 683. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saffarid-dynasty">Saffarids</a> later conquered it from the Zunbils, but the non-Muslim Lawik dynasty remained in Ghazni until the Samanid slave commander Alp-Tegin besieged the citadel in 962 and took the city from Abu Bakr Lawik.</p><p>From 977 to 1163 Ghazni was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a>, which covered much of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. The Ghaznavids carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> and returned with riches looted from temples. Under Sultan Mahmud, who reigned from 998 to 1030, Ghazni was in the early 11th century the most important centre of Persian literature, since he gathered scholars, philosophers and poets at his court to support his claim to royal status in Iran.</p><p>The Ghurids sacked Ghazni, but in 1173 it became their secondary capital and flourished again. It was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> between 1215 and 1221. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1333 he found most of the town in ruins. It stayed under Mughal control until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> invaded in 1738, and then became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a>. British forces partly demolished its fortifications on 23 July 1839 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-afghan-war">First Anglo-Afghan War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ghazni is the capital of Ghazni Province in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, about 150 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>. Its monuments include the Ghazni Citadel, the mausoleum of Sultan Mahmud, the tomb of al-Biruni and the Palace of Sultan Mas'ud III. The two Ghazni Minarets, built on a stellar plan and decorated with geometric patterns, date from the middle of the 12th century and are the surviving elements of the mosque of Bahramshah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ghazni” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It was contested by rival Ghurid successors such as Taj al-Din Yildiz of <b>Ghazni</b> and Nasir al-Din Qabacha of Multan and by resistant Rajput rulers, and its administration rested on personal loyalty, not on institutions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>When Nader Shah died in June 1747, Ahmad secured Kandahar and <b>Ghazni</b>, and a loya jirga, a grand council, met near Kandahar for nine days.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The scholar al-Biruni, brought to <b>Ghazni</b> after Mahmud conquered Khwarazm, learned Sanskrit and wrote a detailed study of Indian religion, science and customs, completed around 1030.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghor, a remote and mountainous region of central Afghanistan between Herat and <b>Ghazni</b>, was among the last parts of the eastern Iranian world to accept Islam, and its people lived in fortified villages under local chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mahmud of Ghazni invaded, annexed Khwarazm and took al-Biruni to <b>Ghazni</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mamunid-khwarazm">Ma'munid Khwarazm</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1260s they formed an independent force in the region of <b>Ghazni</b> and Balkh.</blockquote><a href="/state/qaraunas">Qara'unas</a></li></ul></section>
